= TPMS Lattice Design =

The TPMS Lattice Design page specifies the geometric backbone of every [[Psi-Tech]] metamaterial substrate. The [[Smart Composite Fill]] (B8) specifies what goes into the lattice; this page specifies the lattice itself — its geometry, fabrication route, embedded sensor integration, and thermal management architecture.

The derivation chain is: [[ψ Boundary Conditions]] (A5) specifies the Neumann condition → the Neumann condition requires a material with low ψ loss (high <math>Q_\psi</math>) and high surface-to-volume ratio → the TPMS geometry is the unique class that simultaneously satisfies both constraints with fabricable unit cells at sub-millimetre scale.

== Why TPMS, Not a Conventional Lattice ==

Three properties make triply periodic minimal surfaces the mathematically correct geometry for ψ resonant shells. Each property traces to a specific equation from Branch A.

=== Isotropy — from □ψ mode structure ===

The [[ψ Boundary Conditions]] (A5) page shows that the ψ₀₁₀ mode of the oblate spheroidal [[Psi Stabilizer]] shell has azimuthal symmetry — it is the same at all longitudes. This requires the shell material to have isotropic effective medium parameters: <math>\varepsilon_{\rm eff}</math> and <math>\mu_{\rm eff}</math> must be the same in all directions, or the mode shape will distort away from the target azimuthal symmetry.

TPMS geometries of cubic symmetry (both Gyroid and Schwarz-P belong to cubic space groups Ia3d and Im3m respectively) have isotropic effective medium properties in the long-wavelength limit <math>a \ll \lambda</math>. A conventional octet-truss or body-centred cubic lattice does not — it has strong cubic anisotropy in its effective elastic and electromagnetic tensors.

=== Zero Mean Curvature — from ferrofluid channel stability ===

The [[Smart Composite Fill]] (B8) routes ferrofluid through dedicated microchannels in the TPMS walls for real-time <math>\mu_{\rm eff}</math> control. Ferrofluid in a curved channel experiences a Laplace pressure <math>\Delta P = \gamma (1/R_1 + 1/R_2)</math> where <math>R_1, R_2</math> are the principal radii of curvature and <math>\gamma</math> is the surface tension.

For a minimal surface, the mean curvature <math>H = \frac{1}{2}(1/R_1 + 1/R_2) = 0</math> everywhere by definition. Therefore <math>\Delta P = 0</math> everywhere on the TPMS wall — ferrofluid in a minimal surface channel experiences no Laplace pressure gradient, and therefore does not spontaneously flow or redistribute. This is critical: a non-minimal surface would create pressure-driven ferrofluid redistribution that would spatially vary <math>\mu_{\rm eff}</math> in an uncontrolled way, distorting the ψ mode shape.

=== Maximum Surface-to-Volume Ratio — from αF²μν coupling ===

The [[ψ Transducer]] (C3) page shows that EM-ψ coupling efficiency scales with the volume integral of <math>F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}</math> over the composite. The BST fill (from B8) has the highest <math>F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}</math> response per unit mass of all composite phases. Maximising the interface area between the solid scaffold and the BST fill — at constant overall fill volume — directly increases the coupling integral.

For a given porosity (relative density), TPMS geometries have the highest surface-to-volume ratio of any triply periodic structure. The Gyroid's surface-to-volume ratio at 25% relative density is approximately <math>S/V \approx 2.7/a</math> where <math>a</math> is the unit cell edge length — substantially higher than a sphere-packing (≈ 1.8/a) or a gyroid-like but non-minimal lattice (≈ 2.1/a at the same density).

