== The Level-Set Equations ==

=== Gyroid ===

:<math>\sin x\cos y + \sin y\cos z + \sin z\cos x = t</math>

{{Math_Bridge
| equation_label = Gyroid level-set equation
| equation       = <math>\sin x\cos y + \sin y\cos z + \sin z\cos x = t</math>
| in_english     =
* The equation defines a surface in 3D space — the set of all points <math>(x, y, z)</math> where the left-hand side equals exactly <math>t</math>.
* <math>t = 0</math>: the classical Gyroid minimal surface. The solid region is one side of this surface; the void is the other.
* <math>t > 0</math>: the solid region grows, occupying more volume — higher relative density.
* <math>t < 0</math>: the void region grows — lower relative density.
* Coordinates <math>x, y, z</math> are scaled: one full period of the Gyroid occupies <math>2\pi</math> in each coordinate, corresponding to one unit cell of physical length <math>a</math>. So <math>x = 2\pi X/a</math> where <math>X</math> is the physical coordinate.
* Engineering use: choose <math>t</math> to set the relative density, then generate the STL mesh from the implicit surface.
}}

The Gyroid belongs to space group Ia3d (No. 230) — the highest-symmetry cubic space group with chirality. Its effective medium tensor is isotropic to second order in <math>(a/\lambda)^2</math>. The slight residual anisotropy from higher-order terms can be deliberately aligned with the ψ₀₁₀ mode polarisation axis to increase coupling efficiency.

'''Genus:''' The Gyroid surface has genus 3 per unit cell — it has three topologically distinct "handles." This means the two interpenetrating channel networks it creates are separately connected throughout the entire lattice, enabling independent routing of ferrofluid (in one network) and polymer fill (in the other).

=== Schwarz-P ===

:<math>\cos x + \cos y + \cos z = t</math>

{{Math_Bridge
| equation_label = Schwarz-P level-set equation
| equation       = <math>\cos x + \cos y + \cos z = t</math>
| in_english     =
* Simpler than the Gyroid — three cosine terms, one per axis.
* <math>t = 0</math>: the Schwarz Primitive (P) minimal surface, space group Im3m (No. 229).
* Genus 3 per unit cell, same as Gyroid, but with higher symmetry — the surface has mirror planes along all body-diagonal directions.
* Higher symmetry → more isotropic effective medium tensor → preferred for applications where isotropy matters more than coupling efficiency (e.g., [[Psi Defender]] shield where the ψ field must be attenuated equally in all directions).
}}

=== Density vs. Parameter t ===

For both surfaces, relative density <math>\rho_{\rm rel}</math> (fraction of solid volume) increases with <math>|t|</math>. Approximate relationships (valid for <math>|t| < 1.4</math>):

