! <math>t</math> !! Gyroid <math>\rho_{\rm rel}</math> !! Schwarz-P <math>\rho_{\rm rel}</math> !! Engineering regime
|-
| 0.0 || 50% || 50% || Too dense — minimal void for fill
|-
| 0.3 || 37% || 38% || Upper bound of target window
|-
| 0.4 || 30% || 31% || Mid-range target (Stabilizer)
|-
| 0.5 || 24% || 25% || Lower bound of target window
|-
| 0.7 || 14% || 16% || Too sparse — mechanical integrity compromised
|-
| 1.0 || 6% || 9% || Near percolation threshold — avoid
|}

Target window: <math>t = 0.3</math>–<math>0.5</math>, giving <math>\rho_{\rm rel} = 18</math>–<math>37\%</math>. Within this window, the lattice is mechanically robust under 5g acceleration (relevant to [[Psi-Ops]] field conditions), has adequate void volume for composite infusion, and maintains effective medium validity at the target unit cell size.

== Effective Medium Validity Condition ==

For the TPMS lattice to function as a homogeneous effective medium (rather than a periodic structure with photonic-bandgap-like behaviour), the unit cell size must be much smaller than the relevant ψ wavelength:

:<math>a \ll \lambda_\psi = \frac{2\pi}{k} = \frac{2\pi v_\phi}{\omega}</math>

At the [[Psi Stabilizer]] operating frequency <math>f = 40</math> kHz and with phase velocity <math>v_\phi \approx c = 3\times10^8</math> m/s (massless limit, valid since <math>m_{\rm eff} \ll \omega</math> at this frequency):

:<math>\lambda_\psi \approx \frac{c}{f} = \frac{3\times10^8}{4\times10^4} = 7500\,\text{m}</math>

At 1.2 mm unit cell: <math>a/\lambda_\psi = 1.2\times10^{-3}/7500 \approx 1.6\times10^{-7}</math> — many orders of magnitude below the homogenisation threshold. The effective medium approximation is excellent.

The binding constraint is not the ψ wavelength but the coherence length <math>L_\psi = 1/m_{\rm eff}</math>. The unit cell must be smaller than the coherence length to act as a continuous medium for the static Yukawa field profile:

:<math>a \ll L_\psi \implies a < \frac{L_\psi}{10}</math>

For the [[Psi Stabilizer]] target <math>L_\psi = 20</math> cm: maximum unit cell = 2 cm. The 1.2 mm target satisfies this with a factor of 16 margin.

