== Topology Optimisation ==

The unit cell density parameter <math>t</math> need not be spatially uniform. A graded-density lattice — with <math>t = t(\mathbf{x})</math> varying continuously through the shell — creates a graded-index ψ medium, analogous to a GRIN optical lens.

The SIMP (Solid Isotropic Material with Penalisation) method poses the optimisation as:

:<math>\min_{t(\mathbf{x})} \left[\int_V c_{\rm mech}(t)\,dV + c_{\rm EM}(t)\,dV\right]</math>

subject to:
:<math>\int_V t(\mathbf{x})\,dV \leq V_{\rm max}, \qquad \omega_{010}(t) = \omega_{\rm target}</math>

where <math>c_{\rm mech}</math> is mechanical compliance under acceleration load and <math>c_{\rm EM}</math> is deviation of <math>\varepsilon_{\rm eff}</math> from the target profile.

'''Software:''' nTopology (preferred for TPMS-native topology optimisation), ANSYS Topology Optimization, or custom Python scripts using the <code>optimistix</code> library with autodiff through the Maxwell-Garnett model. The AI writing this page should not attempt to solve the optimisation problem — specify the problem formulation and cite the tools.

'''Practical shortcut for prototyping:''' Uniform <math>t</math> with values from the table above is sufficient for TML-1 and TML-2 validation. Graded-t optimisation is a TML-4 refinement.

== DLP Fabrication Protocol ==

{{Way_It_Works
| title       = Step-by-step DLP fabrication of TPMS ψ resonant shell
| step1_label = Generate the level-set mesh
| step1_text  = Use Python (via <code>scikit-image</code> <code>marching_cubes</code> on the level-set function) or nTopology to generate a watertight STL mesh of the Gyroid at target <math>t</math> and unit cell size <math>a = 1.2</math> mm. Shell dimensions: oblate spheroid 220 mm × 180 mm. Total mesh: approximately 2–5 million triangles at 1.2 mm unit cell — manageable with a modern slicer.
| step2_label = Slice for DLP
| step2_text  = Import STL into Chitubox or PreForm. Layer thickness: 25 μm. Exposure time: calibrate per resin — target 8–12 mJ/cm² cure energy. Supports: internal self-supporting (Gyroid geometry is self-supporting above <math>t = 0.2</math>). Estimated print time at 25 μm layers for the full Stabilizer shell: 14–18 hours.
| step3_label = Resin selection
| step3_text  = Require: HDT (heat deflection temperature) > 150°C to survive the 120°C field-cure step; low shrinkage (< 1% linear) to maintain dimensional accuracy at 1.2 mm unit cell; optical clarity (for UV post-cure through the shell). Candidates: Formlabs High Temp Resin (HDT 238°C, shrinkage 0.4%); Photocentric Magna Clear; Monocure 3D Rapid Clear.
| step4_label = Post-cure
| step4_text  = UV flood cure: 60 minutes at 60°C under 405 nm flood lamp at ≥ 10 mW/cm². This completes polymerisation and relieves internal stress. Do not skip — incompletely cured resin will creep and deform during the 120°C composite cure step.
| step5_label = Embedded conductor traces (Option A — multi-material)
| step5_text  = For HelmKit production: use Voltera nano-silver ink in a direct-ink-writing head mounted alongside the DLP build platform. Print bifilar traces (0.3 mm trace width, 0.2 mm gap) as part of the shell during DLP build. Trace resistivity target: < 5×10⁻⁶ Ω·m before post-process plating.
| step6_label = Embedded conductor traces (Option B — post-process LIG)
| step6_text  = For prototyping: after DLP printing, use a CO₂ laser (10.6 μm, 5W, 100 mm/s scan speed) to scribe graphene traces directly onto the polymer surface via laser-induced graphene (LIG). Achieves ~10⁻⁵ Ω·m resistivity. For high-current bifilar drive: follow with 2 μm electroless copper plating (Transene Copper Gleam PCM bath, 30 min at 45°C).
| step7_label = FBG sensor threading
| step7_text  = Thread pre-written FBG arrays (OFS BraggMETRO or equivalent, 1 FBG per 10 cm, 1550 nm centre wavelength) through dedicated 0.5 mm channels printed into the TPMS walls. One FBG per 10 cm² of shell surface area. Secure with UV-cure adhesive at entry/exit points only — free in the channel elsewhere to minimise cross-sensitivity.
}}

