== Composite Infusion Sequence ==

The three-phase infusion sequence is specified in detail in [[Smart Composite Fill]] (B8). The order is non-negotiable — the three phases have incompatible processing requirements and must be applied sequentially:

# '''Phase 1 — CNT-epoxy:''' Infuse first while all pore channels are open. Partial cure to B-stage.
# '''Phase 2 — BST-PEG-silica-PZT:''' Infuse into remaining pore volume. UV cure.
# '''Full thermal cure:''' 120°C ramp under 10 kV/cm alignment field.
# '''Phase 3 — Ferrofluid:''' Fill dedicated microchannels only. Seal inlets.

The TPMS lattice must be fabricated with dedicated ferrofluid microchannels (0.5 mm diameter, serpentine routing through lattice walls) that are completely isolated from the main pore volume. These channels are specified in the STL mesh as additional hollow passages — not part of the TPMS level-set geometry but added parametrically in the CAD environment before slicing.

== Thermal Management ==

The bifilar coil drive dissipates power in the composite fill. At peak Anchor mode drive (200V, 40 kHz, bifilar coil), estimated total dissipation is 0.5–2 W across the full shell. Maximum permitted shell surface temperature at skin contact: 43°C (ISO 13732-1 burn threshold for 10-minute contact).

The ferrofluid microchannels (Phase 3 above) double as coolant channels. Flow specifications:
* Coolant: EMG-900 water-based ferrofluid (thermal conductivity 0.8 W/m·K, heat capacity 3800 J/kg·K)
* Flow rate: 0.5–2 mL/min (mini peristaltic pump, e.g. Bartels mp6, integrated into HelmKit frame)
* Inlet temperature: ambient
* Maximum outlet temperature: ambient + 8°C
* Thermal model: 1D fin-network finite difference; validate against COMSOL Heat Transfer module

The serpentine channel routing ensures that the temperature gradient across the shell is < 2°C, preventing thermal gradients from producing spatial <math>\mu_{\rm eff}</math> variations in the ferrofluid (which has dM/dT ≈ −0.1 kA/(m·K)).

